{"version": "6.9", "hitCount": 1, "request": {"queryString": "TITLE:\"Rapamycin fed late in life extends lifespan in genetically heterogeneous mice\"", "resultType": "core", "cursorMark": "*", "pageSize": 4, "sort": "", "synonym": false}, "resultList": {"result": [{"id": "19587680", "source": "MED", "pmid": "19587680", "pmcid": "PMC2786175", "fullTextIdList": {"fullTextId": ["PMC2786175"]}, "doi": "10.1038/nature08221", "title": "Rapamycin fed late in life extends lifespan in genetically heterogeneous mice.", "authorString": "Harrison DE, Strong R, Sharp ZD, Nelson JF, Astle CM, Flurkey K, Nadon NL, Wilkinson JE, Frenkel K, Carter CS, Pahor M, Javors MA, Fernandez E, Miller RA.", "authorList": {"author": [{"fullName": "Harrison DE", "lastName": "Harrison", "firstName": "David E", "initials": "DE", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "The Jackson Laboratory, Bar Harbor, Maine 04609, USA. david.harrison@jax.org"}]}}, {"fullName": "Strong R", "lastName": "Strong", "firstName": "Randy", "initials": "R"}, {"fullName": "Sharp ZD", "lastName": "Sharp", "firstName": "Zelton Dave", "initials": "ZD"}, {"fullName": "Nelson JF", "lastName": "Nelson", "firstName": "James F", "initials": "JF"}, {"fullName": "Astle CM", "lastName": "Astle", "firstName": "Clinton M", "initials": "CM"}, {"fullName": "Flurkey K", "lastName": "Flurkey", "firstName": "Kevin", "initials": "K"}, {"fullName": "Nadon NL", "lastName": "Nadon", "firstName": "Nancy L", "initials": "NL"}, {"fullName": "Wilkinson JE", "lastName": "Wilkinson", "firstName": "J Erby", "initials": "JE"}, {"fullName": "Frenkel K", "lastName": "Frenkel", "firstName": "Krystyna", "initials": "K"}, {"fullName": "Carter CS", "lastName": "Carter", "firstName": "Christy S", "initials": "CS"}, {"fullName": "Pahor M", "lastName": "Pahor", "firstName": "Marco", "initials": "M"}, {"fullName": "Javors MA", "lastName": "Javors", "firstName": "Martin A", "initials": "MA"}, {"fullName": "Fernandez E", "lastName": "Fernandez", "firstName": "Elizabeth", "initials": "E"}, {"fullName": "Miller RA", "lastName": "Miller", "firstName": "Richard A", "initials": "RA"}]}, "dataLinksTagsList": {"dataLinkstag": ["altmetrics", "recommended_reviews", "related_data", "wikipedia"]}, "journalInfo": {"issue": "7253", "volume": "460", "journalIssueId": 1637390, "dateOfPublication": "2009 Jul", "monthOfPublication": 7, "yearOfPublication": 2009, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "Nature", "title": "Nature", "essn": "1476-4687", "issn": "0028-0836", "isoabbreviation": "Nature", "nlmid": "0410462"}, "printPublicationDate": "2009-07-01"}, "pubYear": "2009", "pageInfo": "392-395", "abstractText": "Inhibition of the TOR signalling pathway by genetic or pharmacological intervention extends lifespan in invertebrates, including yeast, nematodes and fruitflies; however, whether inhibition of mTOR signalling can extend lifespan in a mammalian species was unknown. Here we report that rapamycin, an inhibitor of the mTOR pathway, extends median and maximal lifespan of both male and female mice when fed beginning at 600 days of age. On the basis of age at 90% mortality, rapamycin led to an increase of 14% for females and 9% for males. The effect was seen at three independent test sites in genetically heterogeneous mice, chosen to avoid genotype-specific effects on disease susceptibility. Disease patterns of rapamycin-treated mice did not differ from those of control mice. In a separate study, rapamycin fed to mice beginning at 270 days of age also increased survival in both males and females, based on an interim analysis conducted near the median survival point. Rapamycin may extend lifespan by postponing death from cancer, by retarding mechanisms of ageing, or both. To our knowledge, these are the first results to demonstrate a role for mTOR signalling in the regulation of mammalian lifespan, as well as pharmacological extension of lifespan in both genders. These findings have implications for further development of interventions targeting mTOR for the treatment and prevention of age-related diseases.", "affiliation": "The Jackson Laboratory, Bar Harbor, Maine 04609, USA. david.harrison@jax.org", "publicationStatus": "ppublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Print-Electronic", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["research-article", "Research Support, U.S. Gov't, Non-P.H.S.", "Journal Article", "Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]}, "grantsList": {"grant": [{"agency": "NIA NIH HHS", "acronym": "AG", "grantId": "AG025707", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NIA NIH HHS", "acronym": "AG", "grantId": "P30 AG013319", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NIA NIH HHS", "acronym": "AG", "grantId": "P30 AG025707", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NIA NIH HHS", "acronym": "AG", "grantId": "U01 AG022303", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NIA NIH HHS", "acronym": "AG", "grantId": "AG13319", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NIA NIH HHS", "acronym": "AG", "grantId": "U01 AG022308", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NIA NIH HHS", "acronym": "AG", "grantId": "AG022307", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NIA NIH HHS", "acronym": "AG", "grantId": "AG022308", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NIA NIH HHS", "acronym": "AG", "grantId": "P30 AG028740", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NIA NIH HHS", "acronym": "AG", "grantId": "AG022303", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NIA NIH HHS", "acronym": "AG", "grantId": "U01 AG022307", "orderIn": 0}]}, "meshHeadingList": {"meshHeading": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Animals"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Mice"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Disease Susceptibility"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Sirolimus", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "abbreviation": "AD", "qualifierName": "administration & dosage"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "abbreviation": "PD", "qualifierName": "pharmacology"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Phosphotransferases (Alcohol Group Acceptor)", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", 